WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
So, my secret's out. I can finally be honest with you.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.280
I'M IN THE FNAF MOVIE!

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.280
OH, IT HAS BEEN A YEAR!

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.000
And not only was I in the FNAF movie;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.400
my scene was the first scene shot of the entire production.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.400
Day one, scene one, I was there.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.600
I also got to meet Scott Cawthon after eight years of us dancing around each other

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.600
for the first time in person.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.200
But you know what? We're gonna get to all that.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.400
First, and most importantly, I just need to apologize.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.800
I'm sorry that I had to lie to you for the better part of the last year

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.000
to make it seem like I wasn't a part of this whole thing.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.400
I'm sorry that there were so many of you that were convinced that I wasn't in it

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.000
and were mad at the production on my behalf.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.600
Trust me, I would've been mad at the production too if I had thought the same thing.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.800
I never wanted to cause any sort of stress or anxiety or bad feelings to any one of you out there,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.800
and honestly, for what it's worth, I cannot tell you how wonderful it was for me to see

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.000
so many of you up in arms defending my right to be a part of this thing.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.200
I see each of you, and I appreciate what you guys were doing.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:00.800
Seriously.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.800
It meant the world to see so many of you care and defend me like that.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:04.600
It was awesome.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:10.200
I just hope that the surprise of me walking out on stage and being revealed to be in this thing

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.800
was worth all the fake-out.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.400
And let me be clear, at the end of the day,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.400
no one from the movie once gave me any sort of instructions on how to handle this whole situation.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.800
No one swore me to secrecy, there was no paperwork,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.800
there were no explicit instructions of what I could and couldn't say.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.400
It was all kind of implied.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.000
Everything else was just kind of on me.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.400
But I also knew that if I didn't cover the movie,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.000
or do, like, live reactions to the trailers on GT Live, things like that,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.800
that you all would probably figure it out.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.800
You would sense that something was off about our content calendar,

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.400
that I was hiding something from you,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.600
and in turn, that would just ruin the production's secret.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.200
And let me tell you, I did not expect them to make my life so incredibly difficult,

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.400
especially when they started throwing YouTuber cameos into the trailers themselves.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:52.400
Thanks for that one there, guys.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.600
Is that Dawko?

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:54.800
No, are you kidding me?

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.000
Are you kidding me?!

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.800
Fun fact, by the way, they actually had me read for that role literally a week before their filming started.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.000
So there I was, awkwardly in my basement at midnight on a Friday,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.200
with Steph filming me reacting to a giant robot bear in the imaginary backseat of my invisible taxi cab.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:11.800
No!

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.400
No!

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.400
Not cool, man!

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:16.600
Not cool!

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.400
Oh, and also, in case you were wondering,

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.200
actually being in the movie kind of made making theories predicting what was going to be in the movie a bit tricky.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.400
I had the whole script, and so basically, unless they rewrote the whole thing,

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.600
I knew basically all the major story beats.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.600
It kind of felt like I would be cheating somehow or spoiling the movie in advance unfairly.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.600
So instead, that film theory prediction of the movie was one of the few times

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.000
where I actually took a complete step back from the channel

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.200
and was almost entirely leaving the script in someone else's hands,

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.400
letting film theory's creative director, Lee,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.000
handle all the heavy lifting based on information that was available via the trailers.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.800
And I gotta say, I was surprised with just how much he got right,

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.600
and also some of the things that he missed. But you know what?

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.000
That's a theory for another day.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.600
Anyway, so yeah, almost exactly one year ago,

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.000
all the way back in November of 2022,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.400
I was contacted by Blumhouse to be a part of the movie,

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.800
or Bad Cupcake, as they were calling it back then.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.400
It was the secret code name, so no one discovered what it was.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.000
A week before flying out, they were still shuffling me around to see what my part would be,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
and on February 1st, day one, scene one, I was in New Orleans to film this thing.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Hey there, welcome to Sparky's.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.200
Okay, but let's get real.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.400
The whole actor thing is kind of crazy.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.600
They're like, no, we will bring the stuff to you, and oh, you can stay in your trailer.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.800
I'm like, it's okay, I can go get my own food.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.400
They're like, no, but you are the actor.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.400
One thing off the bat that I didn't expect was actually the scale of this thing,

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.600
because it was massive.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.000
And you're probably thinking, well, no duh, Matt, it was a movie,

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.400
but I guess that never really registered with me.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.000
I mean, I've done professional theater before,

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.000
I've done things like big MrBeast shoots where he takes over entire football stadiums.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.800
How different could this actually be?

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.600
Well, it was different.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:51.800
It was huge.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.800
I have never seen anything this size before.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.000
The number of people, the coordination.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.000
I had a costume fitting the day before,

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.200
and for my dinky little cameo of three lines in this movie,

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.000
I kid you not, they had me try on 20 different combinations of outfits,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.400
all of which had to then be run by the director and Scott for their approval.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.400
I mean, an entire floor of a building was just full wall-to-wall of clothing,

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.400
hundreds and hundreds of outfits.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.800
Mike alone had practically an entire wall all to himself.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.200
It was actually there that I got to see who else was gonna be a part of this movie up on the wall.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.600
That's where I actually learned that Cory was gonna be a cab driver for the first time.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.200
It was also where I saw that good old Markiplier was supposed to be the security guard

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.200
who gets killed at the very beginning of the movie.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.200
But probably most importantly of all,

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:40.400
it's there that I also saw the withered, dabbing Chica posted on the wall,

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.200
like some sort of guardian angel over the production.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.800
Basically, I was just doing the whole Theorist thing

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.000
by trying to pick up as many small details and Easter eggs as I could

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.800
in what few moments I had left unsupervised.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.000
Funny enough, it turns out the production staff were trying to do the exact same thing right back at me.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.400
From the moment I stepped into my first costume fitting,

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.200
people started to grill me about FNAF.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:01.800
No joke.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.600
Apparently, they had been briefed that I was someone who had studied the franchise a lot,

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:09.800
and so I was getting inundated with all sorts of questions about the type of person that Mike is,

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:12.200
or how I saw William Afton in my mind's eye,

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.000
or whether I could give them information about characters like Abby.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.400
I honestly think it was them trying to get a feel for whether they were on the right track with all of this,

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.000
how the community, as fans, might respond to all of this world that they'd created,

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.400
which honestly is pretty cool.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.200
The same thing happened the next day when we were on location at the shoot,

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.800
where all the other actors in my scene, between setups,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.000
would take time to ask me about how their characters behaved in the games,

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.200
how their role fit into the overarching narrative of the franchise,

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.400
which, I gotta say, was kind of a bummer,

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.400
because all the characters in my scene were just made up for the movie,

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.800
so didn't have a whole lot of detail to impart on them.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.000
So instead of all that, Heather Stewart Masterson,

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.800
uh, she's Aunt Jane in the movie,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.600
she just ended up asking me a lot of questions about the parallels between Abby and Baby from the games,

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:58.600
since that's the one that her character is probably most focused on.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.400
In general, though, it was clear that, across the board,

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.000
everyone was so eager to do the franchise justice.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.200
They wanted to know as much as they could. They wanted to learn;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.200
they wanted to absorb all that information,

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:13.200
and they were proud to be a part of a project and being able to show it off.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.000
The only sad part of it all was that I was so early in this process,

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.200
most of the stuff wasn't even ready for me to see yet.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.600
They really wanted to show me the pizzeria,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.800
but schedule-wise, the first month of production was dedicated to everything happening outside of the pizzeria,

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.600
meaning that the set, it was still being built, it was still in process.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.000
So when they took me to see it, it just got a lot of blank walls.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.000
And don't get me wrong, they were impressive blank walls,

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.400
very big, very majestic blank walls,

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:40.200
but still left a lot to the imagination.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.000
Same thing kind of happened when they showed me the animatronics.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.200
Everything was still locked away because it needed final testing,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:48.200
so the most I got was, like, a little peek through a sliver in the wall.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:49.000
It was awesome.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.400
I think I could make out the murder mask.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.000
I'm like, what's up with that weird Freddy head?

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.200
And they're like, I don't know, creative decision.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.400
But come to think of it, it actually kind of worked out really well,

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.200
because when it came time to react to the trailers when they were being uploaded to YouTube,

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.400
I didn't have to fake anything,

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:05.000
because it was truly my first time getting to see all of this thing finished.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.200
Sure, I knew the storyline of the movie,

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:09.800
uh, I had the whole script in my hands,

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.000
and I knew what part I played in this whole thing,

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.200
but seeing it all, actually getting to experience how it all worked together,

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:16.400
it was my first time.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.000
No fake reactions here, my friends.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.200
And, to be honest, seeing it on camera doesn't do the scale of this whole thing justice.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:25.800
I cannot stress to you how massive this production was.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:31.000
They literally built an entire restaurant inside of an old abandoned Home Depot in New Orleans.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.400
Hallways, rooms, it was a complete restaurant,

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:36.400
which was just mind-blowing to me.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:40.200
Most movies would probably try to do some clever camera tricks or green-screening or something,

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:44.600
but no, they built an entire building from the ground up inside of another building.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:45.800
It was crazy.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.200
As far as my role in all of this, I had a one-day shoot,

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.800
and that actual day I had to wake up at 4 a.m.,

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:54.800
and then find a car that had been left for me in a random parking garage the night before.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.800
Inside the car were explicit driving instructions to a secret location.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:01.400
Really felt like something straight out of a spy movie.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:05.800
From there, I drove an hour out of town to a random diner that had been shut down due to COVID.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:09.000
Once we were on location, they actually gave me my own trailer.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.200
They told me that's not the sort of thing that a day player would normally get,

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.600
but they wanted to give us YouTubers the star treatment,

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:17.400
which, I gotta say, was just, like, incredibly generous of them.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.800
That's right. Got three lines, baby.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.800
This is what I get. It's nice.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.800
This is where I'm gonna be hanging out.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:29.000
This is where the next two episodes of Game Theory will be written, probably.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.800
Inside my trailer, I found this.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.200
This guy right here. Little rubber chicken head. Little pencil topper.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.000
He was in one of the drawers, and so I'm like,

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.000
you know what? I'm gonna work him into my character.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.600
And so, if you see him in the movie, that's it.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:42.000
Little Easter egg for ya.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.000
And from there, it was on the set where, for the next eight hours,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.200
we were working on a single scene.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:48.600
Not one diner scene.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.600
You heard that right. Eight straight hours.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.200
One thing. Why so long?

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:54.800
They had the cameras set up outside.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.200
Then they had to move the cameras inside.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:58.200
Then they had to have the specialty close-up cameras.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:01.600
Then they had to remove half the booth to capture one side of a conversation.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:03.000
Then remove the other half of the booth.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.600
Replace the other half of the booth to capture the other side of the conversation.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.400
Then they had to remove the booth entirely,

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:09.800
so they could put cameras right here and capture a close-up of my face.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.800
There were b-rolls of different tables getting served.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:15.000
There were b-rolls and specialty shots of dishes getting served up at the window.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.200
It was a lot.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:21.400
For what ultimately amounts in the movie to be, like, three minutes of footage.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:24.400
As you might imagine, we all started to get a bit creative

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:26.200
with how we were filling our time with each take.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:27.400
What? What are you doing?

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.600
I'm filming this for eventually when I do the, like,

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:31.400
hey, I was in this movie, and it's behind the scenes.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:32.400
Oh my goodness.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:33.800
Wah! So no pressure.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:35.400
Hey!

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.800
It's a lengthy time between takes as they moved cameras along

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:43.200
also gave me plenty of chances to explore the set for any hints of lore.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.600
And I actually found a lot of really fun details buried in the walls.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:51.000
Like, uh, here's one time where I found the fake diner's registration information.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.800
Here's another one where I found a QR code that I was almost convinced

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.000
was trying to be a start of some new ARG.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.200
Then I learned that it wasn't.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.800
At which point, I decided to call that detail out to the production team

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:05.200
as being anachronistic, since this was meant to be set at a time

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.400
before QR codes became mainstream.

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:08.600
And then they promptly took it down.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:11.400
So, TLDR of this entire video,

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.600
I single-handedly saved the FNAF movie.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.600
By the way, on the subject of small details,

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.000
I don't know if you noticed it in the movie,

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:20.000
but the diner I work in is called Sparky's Diner,

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.600
which is a reference to one of the earliest urban legends from the first FNAF game

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.600
that there was this dog animatronic named Sparky hidden somewhere inside.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.400
The movie is actually full of these sorts of Easter eggs.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:33.200
For instance, my character's official name, right here.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:34.400
It's Ness.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:36.600
I don't know if you'd notice this one in the movie, but it's Ness.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:40.400
And I was told by everyone that Scott specifically called out

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:42.600
that he needed me to be named Ness.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.400
At first, I immediately thought, like,

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.400
oh, this gotta be a reference to Ness from FNAF AR.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:51.200
Or maybe this is another instance of Vanessa from Security Breach.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:54.800
You know, just Scott trolling people by using the same name over and over again.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:57.000
But no, when I met him later in the day,

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.400
he told me it was a reference to Sans as Ness.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:01.400
He was very proud of himself.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:05.400
Speaking of, the man, the myth, the legend himself, Scott Cawthon.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:09.400
The whole time, it was unclear whether Scott would actually be there on set.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.000
It was one of those things that people were like,

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:12.600
we don't know if Scott's gonna be there.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:13.400
He might show up.

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.600
We've asked him if he's gonna show up on the first day.

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:17.000
I thought that was a little bit weird.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.000
I mean, it's your first day of shooting your movie

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.600
that's been like five plus years in the making.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:25.200
I don't know, you'd think that you'd try to make an appearance there or something, right?

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.600
Well, he did, but apparently it was a big surprise.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.800
He flew in special for that day.

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:31.200
It was interesting.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.800
Most of the production crew didn't know who he was.

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:36.600
They just knew him as the VIP on set,

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:39.400
who everyone had to listen to if he asked them to do something,

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.400
but no one actually understood what he was,

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:43.200
that he was the creator of the game.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.800
I think ultimately it winds up being a movie thing, you know,

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:48.600
where he's just responsible for talking to the director

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:51.200
who's then the one who's executing on his creative vision.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.400
So a lot of the rest of the day-to-day staff

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.600
don't really need to know that this is the man

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:58.000
that gave them a reason to be there in the first place.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.000
But, yeah, I got to meet him.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.000
It was great.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.000
He has a good strong handshake,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.200
and he looks you in the eye when you talk,

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.600
which is always the sign of an upstanding person.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.200
He was wearing a classic Zelda t-shirt,

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:10.800
which, big props to that one,

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:14.000
and he was doing a great job, honestly, of directing everyone.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.400
I overheard him consulting some of the costume department

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:20.000
and clearly outlining his vision of what everyone should be wearing,

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.200
which was really cool.

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.400
I think most people would probably have been intimidated

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:26.200
by this massive production all leaning on him,

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:28.600
but it was just really impressive to watch.

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.600
He made sure that I knew about the whole Ness joke

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.200
and also about how he gave the waiter role bonus lines.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.000
In fact, he was so proud of that one line

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.000
that he asked me to go over the top with it,

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.200
like I do in the videos, as part of the scene,

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:44.200
and I did it for one take, and it was weird,

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:48.200
especially with everyone else in the scene taking the scene so seriously,

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:51.000
and so we all decided to keep it a bit more toned down.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:53.400
He took off before I left for the day,

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:54.200
and I said,

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.200
I'll see you again at the premiere,

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:57.800
and, uh, well, there's no premiere,

00:12:57.800 --> 00:12:59.800
so I guess that's the last time I'm ever gonna see him.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:02.400
Oh, and, uh, no, to answer your obvious question,

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:03.800
we did not talk about lore.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:05.200
We both swore that one off.

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:06.800
Instead, we talked about the community

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.600
and how we both expected the community to take this particular script.

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:13.200
In the end, he was just really grateful for me

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:14.400
being able to be a part of the movie,

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.000
and that was mind-blowing to me,

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:17.400
because I'm like, are you kidding?

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.000
I was the one who was lucky.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.200
I've got to be in this mainstream movie.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:22.200
How cool is that?

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:25.400
Growing up, acting was one of my dreams.

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:27.600
To be a performer, that was always, like,

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:31.200
the end goal for me for, like, the first 18 years of my life,

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:34.200
and since then, that goal's obviously changed a lot.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:34.800
I've moved on.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.600
I've done something, honestly, that I'm more proud of here,

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:42.000
where I'm able to make a business and perform as myself instead of as a character,

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.600
but this was, in a lot of ways,

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:47.800
a realization of that dream that baby MatPat had nearly a decade ago.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:51.400
I actually recorded a bit of my thoughts as I was getting ready to leave the set.

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:53.000
It felt important to document.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:54.000
That's a wrap.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.200
I was there.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.000
I was in it.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.200
I'm kind of sad,

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:02.400
but it was cool to see a childhood dream, in some ways, get realized.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:07.000
It was kind of like I got a peek into a multiverse of another life of mine,

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:08.800
and then, that was it.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:10.800
Back home, like, nothing had ever happened.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:14.400
I did keep the pencil topper and the name tag as souvenirs, though,

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:19.800
so, yes, I did steal from the set of FNAF the movie.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.600
For two days, this was just a small window into something that was so big.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:28.800
I got to be a visitor in this massive machine that I hopped off of,

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:31.600
and then, that just continued on without me.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:32.800
Man, let me tell you,

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:38.000
it is surreal to see posters going up in theaters of a movie that you're in.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:40.800
Oh, and can I level with you for a second?

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:43.600
This whole thing, it was so intimidating.

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:46.400
You know how I keep doing videos this year about being in the PGA Tour

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:47.600
or producing a Broadway show,

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:50.200
and how the theme of the year happens to be, like,

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.200
say "yes and" to your life?

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.800
Well, this was actually the first of those things,

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:56.000
because it was at the top of the year.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:59.000
This was the thing that got me started on the whole theme,

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:03.400
because it is one thing to speak as yourself on a couch about things that you know,

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:07.000
and it is something entirely different to walk in as a complete noob

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.600
and say scripted lines as a random character named after a meme

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:14.400
to a crew of seasoned actors while they're stopping public traffic outside

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:18.000
and 50 production staff are slowly zooming a camera into your face.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:22.200
I mean, I've done a lot of performances, but this?

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.000
This was just at another level.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.400
The only thing that I asked the director, Emma, to make sure of

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:31.400
was that I didn't deliver some sort of, like, cringey YouTuber performance.

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:32.400
You know what I mean.

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:36.000
It can't not be cringey when you're saying your, like, own catchphrase, that's kind of silly.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:38.200
But, you know, I just didn't want to bring the movie down.

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:40.400
And to be honest, I think I did all right.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:43.200
Anyway, that right there, my friends, that is my story,

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:47.000
and it feels so good, so good to finally come clean about it.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:50.600
I've wanted to bring you in as a part of this journey for the longest time.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:54.400
In the end, I just, I cannot thank the team there enough

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:56.800
for all their incredible generosity and their patience.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:59.600
And, Scott, thank you for giving me the chance to be a part of this.

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:02.000
But most importantly, thank you.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:05.600
This whole thing, this is our collective victory, right?

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.800
Because without you supporting me, or supporting these channels,

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:12.200
and supporting these wacky theories, none of this would have been possible.

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:15.600
And if you haven't seen the movie, then what the heck are you waiting for?

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:17.800
Watch it, and then go watch it again.

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:19.800
Show them the force of Team Internet

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.800
so that the late-night hosts and the executive producers

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:26.800
finally learn that things from the Internet deserve to be pronounced correctly.

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:28.800
Anyway, in the end, who knows?

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:32.800
Maybe there'll be room in the sequel for the return of a well-meaning cringey waiter

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:35.200
with a love of breakfast and a meme-ish name.

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:38.800
But until then, remember, that's not a theory.

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:41.200
It's just a video. A video for you.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:42.600
Thanks for watching.

